app/data\questions.peds.ep2.js:2527:  image:'q-pd-ep2-640', imgAlt:'A schematic phonocardiogram tracing showing the timing of heart sounds and a murmur across the cardiac cycle.',
app/data\questions.peds.ep2.js:2531:  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> There is flow during systole and diastole suggesting that there is a pressure gradient in both. This makes one think of a shunt between arteries – in this case the aorta to the pulmonary artery across the persistent ductus arteriosus.\n\n---\n\nThis question can only be answered from the figure: the tracing itself is a zigzag that runs continuously across the whole cardiac cycle, straight through the second heart sound, with no silent gap anywhere -- the signature of a *continuous* murmur. A murmur that never stops means there is a pressure gradient driving flow across a communication in both systole and diastole, which only happens where a high-pressure vessel is shunting into a lower-pressure one throughout the cycle -- the aorta into the pulmonary artery through a persistent ductus arteriosus, heard clinically as the \'machinery\' murmur described in `33)Congenital acyanotic heart disease_.txt`.\n\nThis stem and option ladder is shared with `pedep2-car-8`, which prints the identical five options in the identical order and keys atrial septal defect instead. Since the wording never changes, only the figure tells the two apart, and the same reading skill applies across this whole run of five phonocardiogram questions (`pedep2-car-6` through `pedep2-car-10`), each keying a different lesion from the same small set of schematic patterns:\n\n| Tracing pattern | What it means | Lesion keyed |\n|---|---|---|\n| Continuous zigzag through S2, no gap | Flow all cycle long, pressure gradient in systole and diastole | Persistent ductus arteriosus (this entry) |\n| Ejection click, then murmur, quiet P2 | Valvular click plus reduced pulmonary component of S2 | Pulmonary stenosis (`pedep2-car-7`) |\n| No click, fixed and widely split S2 | Right ventricular volume equalised across the respiratory cycle | Atrial septal defect (`pedep2-car-8`) |\n| Dense murmur fills all of systole, no click, no gap before S2 | Pansystolic flow across a fixed low-to-high-pressure defect | Ventricular septal defect (`pedep2-car-9`) |\n| Ejection click, then murmur, quiet A2 | Valvular click plus reduced aortic component of S2 | Aortic stenosis (`pedep2-car-10`) |\n\nWithout that visual cue, every wrong option here is plausible on the stem\'s wording alone: aortic stenosis and coarctation both give an ejection systolic murmur, and mitral stenosis gives a murmur confined to diastole -- none of the three produces a *continuous* trace spanning the whole cycle the way PDA does.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
app/data\questions.peds.ep2.js:2536:  image:'q-pd-ep2-642', imgAlt:'A schematic phonocardiogram tracing showing the timing of heart sounds, an ejection click, and a murmur across the cardiac cycle.',
app/data\questions.peds.ep2.js:2541:  objective:'An ejection click with a soft P2 on a schematic phonocardiogram points to pulmonary stenosis.',
app/data\questions.peds.ep2.js:2545:  image:'q-pd-ep2-644', imgAlt:'A schematic phonocardiogram tracing showing the timing of heart sounds and a murmur across the cardiac cycle.',
app/data\questions.peds.ep2.js:2549:  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> The variation in timing of closure of aortic and pulmonary valves is lost when there is an atrial septal defect. The fixed and widely split second heart sound (often difficult to hear) is due to the right ventricular stroke volume being equal in both inspiration and expiration.\n\n---\n\nThis stem and this exact five-option ladder, in this exact order, is also printed at `pedep2-car-6`, which keys persistent ductus arteriosus from a different figure -- the reading key for the whole family of five tracings, and the table comparing them, is written there. What discriminates this page is its own figure alone: a short diagonal line before the first heart-sound bar, a brief ejection murmur, then two vertical bars sitting close *together* labelled A2 and P2, followed by a separate fourth isolated bar further to the right. A2 and P2 sitting close together and not moving apart across the cycle is the schematic way of drawing a *fixed, widely split* second sound -- normally A2 and P2 drift apart on inspiration as right ventricular filling rises, but in ASD the right ventricle is already volume-loaded through the shunt, so its stroke volume barely changes with respiration and the split stays fixed.\n\nAortic stenosis and coarctation both key to an ejection click pattern in this family (`pedep2-car-10` and, by option content, would key aortic stenosis on this same ladder), which this tracing does not show. Mitral stenosis is a diastolic murmur and would not be drawn as an ejection-phase tracing at all. Persistent ductus arteriosus is the continuous trace at `pedep2-car-6`, not this split-S2 pattern.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
app/data\questions.peds.ep2.js:2550:  objective:'A fixed, widely split second heart sound on the schematic tracing -- A2 and P2 not separating with respiration -- keys atrial septal defect.',
app/data\questions.peds.ep2.js:2554:  image:'q-pd-ep2-646', imgAlt:'A schematic phonocardiogram tracing showing the timing of heart sounds and a murmur across the cardiac cycle.',
app/data\questions.peds.ep2.js:2563:  image:'q-pd-ep2-648', imgAlt:'A schematic phonocardiogram tracing showing the timing of heart sounds, an ejection click, and a murmur across the cardiac cycle.',
app/data\questions.peds.ep2.js:2599:  explanation:'**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> A pansystolic murmur with a thrill at the lower left sternal edge is typical of a VSD. The child may be otherwise well if the defect is small.\n\n---\n\nAnoushka is pink and well-perfused, which already argues against a cyanotic or obstructive-shock lesion, and the murmur\'s location and character do the rest of the work: a pansystolic murmur -- filling the whole of systole, not just an ejection phase -- with a palpable thrill, loudest at the lower left sternal edge, is the classic bedside finding for a ventricular septal defect, the same murmur quality drawn schematically at `pedep2-car-9`. Being otherwise well with a respiratory infection is consistent with a small, restrictive VSD, which `33)Congenital acyanotic heart disease_.txt` describes as often an incidental finding in an asymptomatic child with normal growth.\n\nAtrial septal defect produces an ejection systolic murmur with a fixed split S2 at the upper left sternal edge, not a pansystolic murmur with a thrill lower down. Coarctation of the aorta gives an ejection systolic murmur at the upper sternal edge, with radio-femoral delay, not a lower-sternal-edge pansystolic thrill. Mitral stenosis is a diastolic murmur, heard at the apex, not a pansystolic one at the left sternal edge. Mitral regurgitation does give a pansystolic murmur, but at the apex radiating to the axilla, not at the lower left sternal edge -- the murmur\'s location, not just its timing, is what separates a VSD from a regurgitant mitral valve.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*',
