app/data\questions.ophtho.ep.js:2638:  explanation:"The retina is counted as ten layers, running from the retinal pigment epithelium out to the internal limiting membrane. The layered diagram in the ocular anatomy lecture names them from the vitreal side to the scleral side: internal limiting membrane, nerve fiber layer, ganglion cell layer, inner plexiform layer, inner nuclear layer, outer plexiform layer, outer nuclear layer, external limiting membrane, photoreceptor layer and retinal pigment epithelium (L1,2, slide 31). That is ten names. The same slide also labels the photoreceptor outer segment, Bruch's membrane and the choroid, which are a part of the photoreceptor layer and tissue outside the retina (not taken from the course material).\n\nThe detachment lecture draws the same anatomy: light enters at the nerve fibre layer side and reaches the rods and cones last, so the retina is inverted, and the ganglion, amacrine, bipolar and horizontal cells, the rods and cones, the pigment epithelium and the choroid are all labelled (L20, slide 4). The figure shows a labelled schematic of the retina beside an enlarged cross-section of the wall of the eye, with the same cell types, the choroid and the sclera named. It prints no layer count.\n\n6 layers, 8 layers and 9 layers are wrong because each count is smaller than the ten names on the layered diagram, so each leaves some of them out.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n> The retina consists of 10 layers, from the retinal pigment epithelium to the internal limiting membrane. Each layer plays a specific role in visual processing.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
