app/data\questions.ophtho.js:3516:    explanation:"**Light slows down when it enters a denser medium — it does not speed up — and that slowing is precisely why it bends. B is the false statement.**\n\nThe deck gives one formula in 88 slides and it settles this: **n = c / v**, where n is the index of refraction, c the velocity of light in vacuum and v the velocity of light in the medium (L7,8 p16). Read the equation: for n to be greater than 1, v must be *less* than c. An optically denser medium is by definition one with a higher n, and a higher n means a lower velocity. Density and speed move in opposite directions, always.\n\n**A** is therefore true and is the premise B contradicts. The cornea and lens are optically denser than the air the light arrives from, which is what makes them refract at all; the deck illustrates the principle with its air/water figure, where the ray bends toward the normal on entering the denser medium (L7,8 p13, p17).\n\n**C** is true as the deck teaches it. Its camera analogy names the retina as the film (L7,8 p22), and its emmetropia schematic shows parallel rays converging exactly on the retina (L7,8 p18, p19). The macula is the retinal point where they must land for clear vision, and the astigmatism figures define all five subtypes by where the focal lines sit relative to the **fovea** (L7,8 p67) — so the deck itself uses the macular landmark as the reference plane. Strictly, an eye focuses whatever it is looking at onto the fovea and the rest of the field onto the rest of the retina; \"focused on the macula\" is the statement about the object of regard, and that is how the option is meant.\n\n**D, all of the above**, cannot stand once B is false.\n\nOne clarification the option wording invites: \"optically denser\" is not the same as \"physically denser\". Optical density is refractive index, not mass per volume, and the two do not always track each other. The eye's media happen to be both, so the distinction does not change the answer here. *(No refractive-index value appears anywhere in the deck — its NOT-COVERED register records \"not for cornea, aqueous, lens, vitreous, air or water\" — so the numbers behind this reasoning are not from the course material.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
app/data\questions.ophtho.js:5947:    imgAlt:'Black-and-white schematic of paired visual field charts, printed side by side.',
app/data\questions.ophtho.js:6050:    explanation:"Argyll-Robertson pupils\n\n- Tertiary syphilis with midbrain affection\n- Bilateral (may be asymmetric)\n- **Small irregular pupils with light-near dissociation**\n- Investigations: serologic testing for syphilis and MRI of the brainstem\n\n---\n\n*(The box is the bank's, printed under the key on p.101. It is `ophthalmology.pdf` p.181 verbatim, bullet order included — and its third bullet is the stem word for word.)*\n\n**What light-near dissociation means.** The pupil does not constrict to light but does constrict when the eye fixes on something near. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.180 draws it as a two-panel figure — \"Pupils DO NOT constrict when exposed to bright light\" over \"Pupils DO constrict on a near object\" — and p.181 prints the mnemonic beside a cartoon: **ARP → Accommodation Reflex Present · PRA ← Pupillary Reflex Absent**. The lesion sits in the **pretectal area**, where the light-reflex fibres relay: `L22) Pupil and Visual pathway` slide 8 opens \"**Affection of pretectal area**\" and places the midbrain schematic beside it, then lists \"Small pupils · Light Near dissociation · Neurosyphilis · DM, Alcoholism, Encephalitis\". Its photograph shows both pupils markedly miotic and **irregular in outline**.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong.**\n\n- **B — Horner's syndrome.** Small, yes — but **unilateral**, **round**, and with a **normal light reaction**. Horner is a loss of the sympathetic *dilator* supply, not of the light reflex; `ophthalmology.pdf` p.180 gives its four signs as miosis with anisocoria greater in the dark, ptosis with anhidrosis, enophthalmos and congenital heterochromia. Nothing there is dissociated and nothing there is irregular.\n- **D — pontine haemorrhage.** Small pupils, and famously so — `ophthalmology.pdf` p.179 files \"Pontine haemorrhage\" under **pin-point** pupil beside morphine and organophosphorus poisoning, and `L22` slide 3 lists it under Neurologic causes of miosis. But those pupils are round and the patient is comatose; there is no near reflex to dissociate in an unconscious person, and nothing makes them irregular.\n- **A — Adie's pupil.** The classic mimic, because it is the other cause of light-near dissociation — but its pupil is **large**, unilateral and tonic, and it is the exact opposite of small. ⚠️ **Adie's pupil is absent from every cached source in this module, and that was measured three ways**: `L22`'s own not-covered register, item 1, records that the deck \"names exactly three pupil syndromes — Marcus Gunn, Argyll Robertson and Horner, plus Dorsal Mid Brain Syndrome. **Adie appears on none of the 25 slides**\"; `ophthalmology.pdf` ch.14's register lists \"Adie's tonic pupil / Holmes-Adie syndrome — **not mentioned anywhere**\" as its first pupil gap; and ch.1's register lists it too. The description of it above is therefore **not taken from the course material**.\n- **The fourth cause the module does teach**, and the one worth pairing with this key: **dorsal midbrain syndrome**, `ophthalmology.pdf` p.180's \"3 Ls\" — **L**ight-near dissociation, **L**oss of upgaze, **L**id retraction (Collier sign) — with hydrocephalus, midbrain compression, multiple sclerosis, stroke and midbrain haemorrhage as its causes and MRI mandatory. `L22` slide 9 gives it \"Pineal body tumors, Stroke, MS\" plus convergence-retraction nystagmus. Both it and Argyll-Robertson are pretectal, which is why both dissociate; the pupil **size and shape** is what tells them apart.\n\n**⚠️ Two misprints in the printed option list**, repaired above and recorded here: option A prints \"**Adies**\" for *Adie's*, option B prints \"**Homer's**\" for *Horner's*.",
app/data\questions.ophtho.js:11653:  image: 'q-op-gg-ee6-32', imgAlt: 'Colour schematic drawing of four eyes in a 2x2 grid, with a small torch drawn beaming at one eye of each row', imgEssential: true,
