app/data\questions.ent.js:6094:  source:'ENT QB p.6 (Ear ch.2, Q31); key p.8. **⚠️ THE PRINTED EXPLANATION ANSWERS A DIFFERENT QUESTION** — the stem asks about perichondritis and the box describes haematoma treatment. Recorded rather than silently dropped, per `CLAUDE.md` §4: the bank is unreliable here even though its key is right. **The key is left exactly as printed (B)** — it is not disputed, it is grounded in `L17,18) Otorrhea and otalgia.pdf`. Only two options are printed; the schema permits 2. Cross-reference: `entqb-ear2-30` and `entqb-ear2-46`.' },
app/data\questions.ent.js:10122:  explanation:"**The cricoid cartilage** (with the arytenoid and the aryepiglottic fold above it) forms the **medial** wall of the pyriform fossa; the **thyroid cartilage and thyrohyoid membrane** form the **lateral** wall.\n\n**Picture the fossa for what it is: a gutter running down each side of the larynx.** The larynx bulges backwards into the hypopharynx, and the recess left on either side of it channels food around the airway into the oesophagus. So the wall nearer the midline is **laryngeal**, and the wall further out is the **thyroid ala**.\n\n| Wall | Formed by |\n|---|---|\n| **Medial** | **aryepiglottic fold, arytenoid and CRICOID cartilage** |\n| **Lateral** | **thyroid cartilage** and the thyrohyoid membrane |\n| Above | pharyngoepiglottic fold |\n| Below | narrows into the oesophageal inlet (apex) |\n\n`L1.1) Surgical anatomy of the larynx.pdf` shows exactly this on its coronal schematic — piriform sinus lateral to the larynx, with hyoid, thyroid cartilage, ventricle and cricoid labelled down the midline — and `L1.2) Anatomy of the pharynx.pdf` labels the *\"Piriform fossae\"* on its endoscopic *\"PARTS OF HYPOPHAYNX\"* view, either side of the post-cricoid region.\n\n**Why the fossa earns three questions in this chapter.** It is the commonest site for an impacted **fish bone** (`entqb-thr1-28`); it is the commonest site of **hypopharyngeal carcinoma**, which stays silent in the gutter until it presents late with **referred otalgia** via the vagus (`entep-throat-103`); and the **internal branch of the superior laryngeal nerve** runs beneath its mucosa — the anatomical basis of that referred pain, and the target of the traditional pyriform-fossa local anaesthetic block.\n\n**The odd distractor.** *\"Cricoid membrane\"* is not a structure — the membranes here are the **cricothyroid** and **thyrohyoid**. Recorded as printed.\n\nWritten for this bank — ENT QB prints no explanation here.",
app/data\questions.ent.js:10124:  source:"ENT QB.pdf p.88 (Throat ch.1, Q13); key p.91 — no explanation box. The *\"Piriform fossae\"* are labelled on the *\"PARTS OF HYPOPHAYNX\"* slides of `L1.2) Anatomy of the pharynx.pdf` and on the coronal schematic of `L1.1) Surgical anatomy of the larynx.pdf`; **neither deck lists the fossa's walls in prose**, so the boundary table is tagged in the explanation. **⚠️ Option b, \"Cricoid membrane\", names no real structure**; transcribed as printed. Cross-reference: `entqb-thr1-16`, `entqb-thr1-28`, `entep-throat-103`." },
app/data\questions.ent.js:10188:  source:"ENT QB.pdf p.89 (Throat ch.1, Q21); key p.91 — no explanation box. **⚠️ STEM QUALIFIED — the printed stem reads only \"if vocal cords are in paramedian position\"**, which is answerable as *hoarseness* (offered as option d) if the lesion is unilateral; *(bilaterally)* has been added because this bank's own `entqb-thr1-34` keys bilateral abductor paralysis for stridor. Nothing else altered. `L1.1) Surgical anatomy of the larynx.pdf` shows RLN transection versus non-transection schematically but **names no cord positions and gives no voice outcomes**, so the position table is tagged in the explanation. Confirmed from the other side by `entep-throat-4` (p.1358). Cross-reference: `entqb-thr1-19`, `entqb-thr1-22`, `entqb-thr1-34`." },
app/data\questions.ent.js:10194:  explanation:"**When BOTH nerves are cut** — the recurrent laryngeal *and* the superior laryngeal. That is a lesion of the **vagus trunk**, above the origin of both branches.\n\n**Why it takes both.** Each nerve still pulls the cord somewhere:\n\n- Cut the **RLN** alone and the **cricothyroid survives** (external branch of the SLN). It still tenses the cord and holds it near the midline — the **paramedian** position of `entqb-thr1-21`.\n- Cut the **SLN** alone and only pitch suffers (`entqb-thr1-25`).\n- Cut **both** and nothing is left to move or tense the cord. It settles halfway, slack, in the **intermediate or cadaveric** position — the position of a cord with no innervation at all, which is where the name comes from.\n\n| Lesion | Position | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| Unilateral RLN | paramedian, one side | hoarseness |\n| Bilateral RLN | paramedian, both | **stridor**, near-normal voice |\n| Bilateral SLN | near normal | loss of high notes |\n| **RLN + SLN (vagal trunk) — the key** | **cadaveric** | **aphonia and aspiration** |\n\n**The clinical signature is aspiration**, and it is what makes this the dangerous palsy. The cord cannot meet its fellow, so the glottis never seals: the patient is **hoarse or aphonic, with a weak bovine cough, and inhales fluids**. Bilaterally it is life-threatening.\n\n**Where the lesion must be.** Both branches leave the vagus in the neck, so a lesion taking out both sits **at or above the skull base** — a jugular foramen tumour, a skull-base metastasis, a high vagal schwannoma, or a neck dissection injuring the trunk. **This bank asks exactly that as a vignette at `entqb-thr1-39`**: hoarseness and aspiration with an osteolytic skull-base lesion, keyed **cadaveric**. Read the two together — Q22 gives the rule, Q39 applies it.\n\n**Note: `L1.1) Surgical anatomy of the larynx.pdf` shows only a transected-versus-bruised RLN schematic and names no cord positions at all**, so the table above is tagged as outside the course material.\n\nWritten for this bank — ENT QB prints no explanation here.",
app/data\questions.ent.js:10196:  source:"ENT QB.pdf p.89 (Throat ch.1, Q22); key p.91 — no explanation box. `L1.1) Surgical anatomy of the larynx.pdf` labels both nerves and shows the transection/non-transection schematic, but **names no cord positions and gives no voice outcomes**; the position table and the skull-base localisation are tagged in the explanation. Applied as a vignette by this bank at `entqb-thr1-39`. Cross-reference: `entqb-thr1-20`, `entqb-thr1-21`, `entqb-thr1-25`." },
app/data\questions.ent.js:10290:  explanation:"**Bilateral abductor paralysis.** The combination *stridor **with a normal voice*** is the signature, and it sounds contradictory until you picture the cords.\n\n**Both abductors are dead, so both cords sit paramedian — just off the midline.** That is:\n\n- **a terrible airway** — the chink between them is too small to breathe through, so the patient has **inspiratory stridor** and dyspnoea on any exertion\n- **an excellent voice** — the cords are already close together, so phonation needs almost no adduction and sounds near-normal\n\n**The one muscle responsible** is the posterior cricoarytenoid, the sole abductor (`entqb-thr1-19`), supplied by the recurrent laryngeal nerve. Bilateral RLN injury — classically **total thyroidectomy** — takes out both. It is the reason cord function is checked before and after thyroid surgery.\n\n**The full grid, which this bank tests four times over the same option list:**\n\n| Lesion | Cord position | Airway | Voice |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Bilateral ABDUCTOR — the key** | **paramedian, both** | **STRIDOR** | **near normal** |\n| Bilateral adductor | cannot approximate | adequate | **aphonia + aspiration** — `entep-throat-36` (p.1436) |\n| Unilateral abductor | paramedian, one side | adequate | hoarse, often compensates |\n| Unilateral adductor | one cord bowed | adequate | **breathy, weak** — `entep-throat-33` (p.1428) |\n\n**⚠️ Read the two words in the stem, not the first one.** *Stridor* alone would also fit a bilateral **adductor** lesion in the sense of airway compromise; it is **\"with normal voice\"** that forces the abductor answer, because any adductor problem wrecks the voice by definition. This bank prints three questions off this identical option list — Q34, Q35 and Q36 — and the stem is the only thing that separates them.\n\n**Management, since this is an emergency:** secure the airway first (intubation, or tracheostomy if it will not settle), then wait — a nerve bruised rather than divided may recover over months. Definitive options for a permanent palsy trade voice for airway: **cordotomy, arytenoidectomy or lateralisation**, all of which widen the glottis and coarsen the voice.\n\n**Note: `L1.1) Surgical anatomy of the larynx.pdf` shows only a transected-versus-bruised RLN schematic**; the position-and-voice grid and the management are tagged as outside the course material.\n\nWritten for this bank — ENT QB prints no explanation here.",
app/data\questions.ent.js:10292:  source:"ENT QB.pdf p.90 (Throat ch.1, Q34); key p.91 — no explanation box. **⚠️ NOT A DUPLICATE of `entep-throat-33` or `entep-throat-36`, which share this exact option set** — stages B, C and F pair all three at or near 1.00, but the keys differ and only the stem separates them; the other two are folded printings of this bank's Q36 and Q35 respectively, while Q34 is new. `L1.1) Surgical anatomy of the larynx.pdf` labels the posterior cricoarytenoid and shows the RLN transection schematic but **names no cord positions and gives no voice outcomes**; the grid and management are tagged in the explanation. **⚠️ THE BANK REPRINTS THIS QUESTION ITSELF at `ENT QB.pdf` p.129 (Throat ch.5, Q293); key p.131** — identical stem and identical key, the same four options in a different order, no box. A **within-bank reprint takes no `alsoIn`**; it is simply held once, here. Cross-reference: `entqb-thr1-19`, `entqb-thr1-21`, `entqb-thr1-22`, `entep-throat-4` (p.1358), `entqb-thr5-294` (p.129) on congenital cord palsy." },
app/data\questions.ent.js:18172:  explanation:'**The sixth arch — the lower half of `L12`’s two-line development slide.**\n\n`L12) Pediatric Laryngeal Lesions.pdf`:\n\n> **Development of Larynx — Embryonic phase (0-8 wks.). Organogenesis:**\n> *"4th arch → Supraglottic part of the larynx"*\n> *"**6th arch → Glottic & Subglottic part of the larynx**"*\n\n**Note the wording.** The bank writes *"glottic and **infraglottic**"* where the deck writes *"Glottic & **Subglottic**"*. They are the same region under two names; `L1.1) Surgical anatomy of the larynx.pdf` uses **"Infraglottic space"** on its sagittal-section slide, so both terms are in the course’s own vocabulary.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why it fails |\n|---|---|\n| **4th branchial arch** | Gives the **supraglottis** — the immediately preceding question (`enthd-thr-546`), printed with the identical four options in the identical order and keyed **A**. **The option block is shared; the stems and keys are not** |\n| **5th branchial arch** | Leaves no derivative in humans — it either fails to form or regresses, which is why arch numbering skips from 4 to 6. *(Not taken from the course material; `L12` names only the 4th and the 6th.)* |\n| **None of the above** | Falls with option C being printed on the slide |\n\n**⚠️ Why this half of the larynx behaves differently from the other half, and it follows from the embryology.** The sixth-arch territory is supplied by the **recurrent laryngeal nerve**, which is why:\n\n| Consequence | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| **Motor supply** | The RLN supplies **every intrinsic muscle except the cricothyroid** — `L1.1` labels this explicitly. Cricothyroid is fourth-arch territory and keeps the **external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve** |\n| **Sensation** | Below the cords the RLN; above them the **internal branch** of the superior laryngeal nerve. The sensory watershed sits exactly on the developmental seam |\n| **Cord position after injury** | `L1.1`’s schematic — *"Transection of recurrent laryngeal nerve → cords drawn **ABDUCTED**"* — and the whole of vocal cord paralysis follows from a sixth-arch nerve with a long, exposed course |\n\n*(The arch-to-nerve correlation is **not taken from the course material**; `L1.1` prints the nerve supply and `L12` prints the arches, and neither joins them.)*\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** The two arches also explain the airway’s narrowest point: the **cricoid**, a sixth-arch derivative, is the only **complete cartilaginous ring** in the airway, so the 4.5 mm infant subglottis cannot expand when it swells (`enthd-thr-545`).\n\nCross-reference `enthd-thr-546`, `enthd-thr-545` and `entqb-thr8-394`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
app/data\questions.ent.js:18174:  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.142 (Throat ch.3, Pediatric laryngeal lesions) — **content chapter `ent-pharanat`.** Grounded in `L12) Pediatric Laryngeal Lesions.pdf` (Development of Larynx slide) and `L1.1) Surgical anatomy of the larynx.pdf` (the term "Infraglottic space"; cricothyroid supplied by the external branch of the SLN and not by the RLN; the RLN transection schematic showing abducted cords; the cricoid drawn as a complete ring). ⚠️ **The 5th arch’s fate and the arch-to-nerve correlation are on no cached ENT slide and are tagged as outside the course material.** **Q546 and Q547 print the SAME four options in the SAME order with different stems and different keys — a deliberate pair, not a duplicate.** Cross-reference: `enthd-thr-546`, `enthd-thr-545`, `entqb-thr8-394`.' },
